```kotlin
package com.bookreads.api.di

import com.bookreads.api.BookApi
import com.bookreads.api.LeaderboardRepository
import com.bookreads.api.SessionRepository
import com.bookreads.api.SessionSyncService
import com.bookreads.api.UserRepository
import io.ktor.client.HttpClient
import io.ktor.client.plugins.contentnegotiation.ContentNegotiation
import io.ktor.serialization.kotlinx.json.json
import kotlinx.serialization.json.Json
import org.koin.core.module.Module
import org.koin.dsl.module

expect val httpClientModule: Module

val commonApiModule: Module = module {
    single {
        HttpClient {
            install(ContentNegotiation) {
                json(Json { ignoreUnknownKeys = true })
            }
        }
    }
    single { BookApi(get()) }
    single { UserRepository(get()) }
    single { SessionRepository(get()) }
    single { LeaderboardRepository(get()) }
    single { SessionSyncService(get(), get()) }
}
```

NOTE: `commonApiModule` provides `HttpClient` directly using `HttpClient {}` with no engine — Ktor auto-selects the engine. The `expect val httpClientModule` is empty on each platform (module {}). This avoids platform-specific engine imports in commonMain while still allowing platforms to override if needed.

Wait, actually let me reconsider. The `HttpClient {}` without explicit engine will fail to compile in KMP because there's no default engine on all platforms. We need to use expect/actual for the engine.

REVISED approach: Remove `HttpClient` from `commonApiModule`. Use `expect val httpClientModule` to provide `HttpClient` on each platform.

